{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\nOn 2026-11-18 a `make verify` of an unrelated branch (worktree a2-win-portability, commit b1216d0cda3, rebased on 9ddb035cf59) failed `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py::test_byte_total_consistent_under_concurrent_populate_evict` at `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py:188`: `assert 312 == 365` (`cache.byte_total(store)` against `_real_store_bytes(store)`). The host was running a second `make verify` at the same time. The test passed 3 of 3 times rerun alone. The branch does not touch `src/rebar/_snapshot/` (its only POSIX-visible change routes `fsutil.atomic_write` through a helper that is exactly `os.replace` off Windows).\n\n## Hypotheses (unproven)\n- The running byte counter (`cache.add_bytes`, under `interprocess_lock`) and the populate/evict paths disagree on what a racing populate or evict adds or frees (for example a populate that loses the single-flight race, or `_evict`'s `exclusive_size` measured before a concurrent hardlink change).\n- The assertion reads the walk while a populate is still finishing.\n\n## Root cause (reproduced deterministically on 2026-11-19)\nThe first hypothesis holds. The running total trusts each file's `st_nlink` at the moment one side measures it, and nothing orders that measurement against the hardlinks a donor build (`delta_tree.materialize_via_donor`) creates from a published entry. The failing interleaving: the evictor's `_evict(A)` measures `exclusive_size(A)` while file X has one link, so it will subtract X; a populater then builds the adjacent SHA B by hardlinking X from A, publishes B, and measures `exclusive_size(B)` while X has two links, so it adds nothing for X; the evictor then renames and deletes A and subtracts. X stays on disk in B and is counted nowhere. The observed `312 == 365` is a 53-byte undercount, the size of the fixture's `f3.txt`.\n\nForced single-threaded (scratch script; the evictor's `exclusive_size` call runs `cache.acquire(B)` before returning, then `_evict` continues): `byte_total 7` against an independent walk of `110`, every run. 25 unforced reruns of the test on an idle host all passed, which is why the race needs host load to show.\n\nThe same defect has 2 sibling interleavings: (a) B hardlinks X from A, the evictor measures A (X has two links, frees 0) and deletes A, then B measures X at one link and adds it again, an overcount; (b) B publishes, C hardlinks from B before B measures, so both count X as shared, an undercount.\n\n## Approach\nHold one invariant: outside the store's accounting lock, a file's link count counts only links from published entries. Every step that changes a published file's link count, and every measurement that reads one, happens under that lock.\n\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/cache.py`: add `accounting_lock(root)`, which returns the existing `interprocess_lock` on `<root>/locks/bytes.total.lock` (no new lock file). `add_bytes` takes it and delegates to a held-lock variant that callers already holding the lock use. `acquire` stops adding bytes itself, because the publish step now accounts (next bullet).\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`: both populate paths (`materialize` for code entries, `materialize_tickets` for ticket entries) go through one helper. It runs the donor attempt (hardlinks plus delta rewrite) and, when the donor build succeeds, the publish rename and the `exclusive_size` increment in a single lock hold. A full materialization builds outside the lock, because its files are fresh inodes under `tmp/` that no other build can link, and takes the lock only to publish and account. Only the build whose rename published the entry adds bytes; a build that lost the publish race adds nothing.\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py`: `_evict` holds the lock from `exclusive_size` through the rename to trash, the `rmtree`, and the decrement, so the trash copy's links are gone before the next measurement. `startup_sweep` holds it across its authoritative walk and reset, so a populate cannot land between the two.\n- `docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md`: add the invariant to the \"Sharing changes what the byte total may be built from\" section.\n\nRejected alternatives: having a populate add only the bytes it wrote fresh (still drifts when a donor build that already holds links is discarded); a per-entry lock (a link count is shared across entries, so the state is store-wide).\n\nCost: donor-path populates serialize with each other and with eviction for the length of a delta build (the hardlinks, `read-tree`, and a `checkout-index` of the changed paths). Full materializations still build concurrently.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/cache.py`\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py`\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`\n- `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py`\n- `docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md`\n\n## Testing\nAdd 3 deterministic interleaving tests to `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py`, one per interleaving above. Each test pauses one actor at the suspected window and runs the other actor in a second thread. The paused actor resumes once the second thread has either finished (the race happened, as on the current code) or is waiting on `cache.accounting_lock` (the fix excluded it). A spy on `cache.accounting_lock` reports the wait, so no sleep or timing bound decides the outcome; a generous join bound only turns a hang into a loud failure. Each test then asserts `cache.byte_total(store)` equals the independent inode walk `_real_store_bytes(store)`.\n1. Evict measures, then a populate links from the entry being evicted: pause point is the evictor's `exclusive_size` call.\n2. A populate links from a donor, then the donor is evicted before the populate measures: pause point is the populater's `delta_tree._link_tree_paths` return.\n3. A populate publishes, then a second populate links from it before the first measures: pause point is the first populater's `exclusive_size` call.\nRun each against the pre-fix tree first and record the failures on this ticket. The existing `test_byte_total_consistent_under_concurrent_populate_evict`, `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py`, and `tests/unit/test_snapshot_cache.py` must stay green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The interleaving that produces the drift is reproduced deterministically and recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] The byte total matches the store walk under that interleaving, with a regression test.\n- [ ] The 3 interleaving tests in `tests/unit/test_snapshot_janitor.py` fail on the pre-fix tree and pass with the fix (checked by running them on both trees; results recorded on this ticket).\n- [ ] `make verify` passes on the change."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795144907681855001,"uuid":"0ba0fb24-8d8e-4dc8-b310-360e32ce5d7f"}